    "blogtextliquid_ra7Awx": {
      "type": "blogtextliquid",
      "custom_css": [],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>Dry skin is the biggest culprit. Scent needs something to cling to, and bare, under-hydrated skin is essentially a sponge for alcohol. It drinks the carrier and lets the fragrance oils evaporate into the ether in two hours.</p><p>A simple 12-second routine before you spray can double or triple the wear time of any fragrance in your collection. Here is how to lock down your scent from morning commute to late evening.</p>"
      }
    },
    "blocks_ji7beH": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "blog_editorial_text_section_zNd6WG": {
          "type": "blog--editorial-text-section",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The Three Things That Actually Make A Difference",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_size_mobile": 28,
            "heading_line_height": 1.1,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_margin_bottom": 30,
            "heading_margin_bottom_mobile": 25,
            "column_1_text": "<p><strong>Moisturise first.</strong> It sounds almost too simple, but applying an unscented or lightly scented moisturiser creates a lipid barrier on your skin. Perfume oils bind to fats and lipids, slowing down evaporation by over 60%.</p><p>Focus on your pulse points: the neck, wrists, and behind the ears where your natural circulation provides subtle, steady warmth.</p>",
            "column_2_text": "<p><strong>Do not spray your clothes instead of your skin.</strong> The logic feels sound in the moment, particularly with delicate fabrics, but clothes do not generate body heat. Without warmth, the fragrance never blooms through its top notes into its heart.</p><p>A mix is best: skin for projection and character, collar or scarf for 24-hour endurance.</p>",
            "body_size": 17,
            "body_size_mobile": 15,
            "body_line_height": 1.9,
            "body_color": "#2D2A26",
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "column_gap": 55,
            "column_gap_mobile": 30,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "padding_top": 60,
            "padding_bottom": 60,
            "padding_horizontal": 40,
            "padding_top_mobile": 40,
            "padding_bottom_mobile": 40,
            "padding_horizontal_mobile": 20
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "blog_editorial_text_section_zNd6WG"
      ],
      "name": "Editorial text section",
      "settings": {}
    },
    "1778690749e24fd023": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "blog_narrative_content_9nTtAA": {
          "type": "blog--narrative-content",
          "settings": {
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "section_padding": 60,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "heading": "<p>The Concentration Question: EDT, EDP, And Why It Matters</p>",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_spacing": 25,
            "box_color": "#1C1917",
            "box_radius": 15,
            "box_padding": 45,
            "box_spacing": 35,
            "box_paragraph_1": "<p>EDP (Eau de Parfum) contains a higher concentration of fragrance oil than EDT (Eau de Toilette), which means it evaporates more slowly and stays close to the skin for longer.</p><p>Parfum, or Extrait, is more concentrated still. At Scentspired, our fragrances sit at high Eau de Parfum and Extrait concentrations (20% to 30% oil), meaning you get proper longevity built into the formula.</p><p><strong>Fierce</strong> and <strong>Addiction</strong> are crafted specifically for British weather, clinging through rain, wind, and dry indoor heating.</p>",
            "box_paragraph_2": "",
            "box_paragraph_3": "",
            "box_text_color": "#F5EFE6",
            "box_text_size": 17,
            "box_text_line_height": 1.9,
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "subheading": "<p>A word on olfactory fatigue (nose blindness)</p>",
            "subheading_size": 42,
            "subheading_color": "#1A1816",
            "subheading_spacing": 40,
            "bottom_text": "<p>Before you spray yourself for the fifth time before a meeting, remember that your nose adapts to continuous scents within twenty minutes. Just because you cannot smell your perfume does not mean the person standing next to you cannot.</p><p>Ask a trusted friend before reapplying!</p>",
            "bottom_text_size": 17,
            "bottom_text_color": "#2D2A26",
            "bottom_text_line_height": 1.9
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "blog_narrative_content_9nTtAA"
      ],
      "settings": {}
    },
